+0.02\u00b0F per decade, a range (\u22120.07 to +0.11) that includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1984, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.68\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.38 to +0.97) and highs have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.65\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.34 to +0.96). The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months. Highs rose in 1 of 12 months; June fell.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Longview's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.30 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 46.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1991 (72.76 in); the driest, 2010 (26.82 in). Longview almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.9 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 14 daily record highs, 1 daily record low"}}